{"event_id":"ece69c7b-fa8c-4301-8989-11c77aba080e","event_type":"verdict","timestamp_ns":1041841846770267,"wall_time_iso":"2026-10-21T22:10:24.147081+00:00","payload":{"step_id":"place_line_green","verdict":"valid_variation","claimed_outcome":null,"clause_results":[{"field":"slip_distance_m","comparison":"<=","threshold":0.02,"observed":0.020450688477214138,"pass":false,"clause_id":null,"source":"instruction"}],"explanation":"The slip_line_green monitor fired at 0.02045m, just over the 0.02m grasp_slip_tolerance_m. Evidence window around the fire (object_poses for line_green vs frames for tcp, ~1.95s span during the final descent into line_green_placement) shows the object-to-TCP relative offset growing smoothly and monotonically in z (from about -0.0676m to -0.0542m) in lockstep with the TCP's own planned descent (TCP z fell from 0.142 to 0.128 over the same window while the object's z fell much less, from 0.0745 to 0.0742). There is no sudden jump or discontinuity characteristic of a dropped or shifting part; the pattern is consistent with the part making contact with the placement slot/fixture and its downward travel being arrested while the TCP continued along its commanded trajectory for a few more millimeters -- i.e. the grasp absorbing intended seating contact rather than losing hold of the part. The final seated pose for line_green was within its registry position (0.0105m vs 0.015m tolerance) and orientation (~3 deg vs 10 deg tolerance) tolerances, and the part was confirmed still gripped at release. Classified as a valid variation: a real but physically explained departure from the nominal no-relative-motion baseline, not a grip failure."},"subscriber_count":2,"seq":19}
